                <div class="faq-expanded__list">
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-1">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is actuarial valuation and who needs it?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Estimating present value of future employee benefit liabilities (gratuity, leave, pension) using PUCM. Any company/LLP/establishment with 10+ employees needs valuation of at least gratuity (Payment of Gratuity Act 1972 + Ind AS 19/AS 15). Even companies with LIC policies require separate actuarial valuation for accounting.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-2">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Is actuarial valuation mandatory for gratuity?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes. Payment of Gratuity Act 1972 applies to 10+ employees. Ind AS 19/AS 15 mandate liability recognition based on actuarial PUCM valuation. Provision from first year of service - even before 5-year vesting. Missing valuation = audit qualification.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-3">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">How much does actuarial valuation cost in Mumbai?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Single scheme (gratuity) small company: Rs 5,000-10,000. Mid-size: Rs 10,000-20,000. Multiple schemes: Rs 15,000-40,000. Full Ind AS 19 with sensitivity: Rs 25,000-75,000. Dual-standard (Ind AS 19 + IAS 19/ASC 715): Rs 40,000-1,50,000. Quarterly: Rs 5,000-25,000/quarter.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-4">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the difference between AS 15 and Ind AS 19?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Both require PUCM. Key difference: Ind AS 19 → actuarial gains/losses in OCI (never recycled to P&L). AS 15 → directly to P&L. Ind AS 19 requires more disclosures: sensitivity analysis, maturity profile, expected contributions. Ind AS 19 for listed + qualifying; AS 15 for others.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-5">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Do companies with LIC policy still need actuarial valuation?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes. LIC provides plan asset fund value. Actuarial valuation determines total DBO (obligation). Net liability (DBO minus LIC fund) must be recognised in Balance Sheet. DBO typically exceeds LIC fund value, creating unfunded gap. LIC certificate is not a substitute for actuarial report.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-6">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What data is needed for actuarial valuation?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Employee-wise: DOB, DOJ, salary (Basic+DA for gratuity), designation, status. Leave balances for leave valuation. Plan assets (LIC policy/trust fund). Benefit scheme rules. Previous actuarial report. Benefits paid + joiners/exits during year. Patron validates all data before computation.</p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>


            <!-- Quick Answers -->
            <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:32px;">
                <p><strong>Quick Answers</strong></p>
                <p><strong>Actuarial valuation kya hota hai?</strong> Employees ko future mein milne wali gratuity, leave, pension ki liability ka present value. Actuary PUCM method se calculate karta hai. 10+ employees wali har company ko zaroori hai.</p><p><strong>LIC policy hai toh bhi chahiye?</strong> Haan - LIC fund value plan assets hai. Actuarial valuation se DBO nikalti hai. DBO minus LIC = net liability jo Balance Sheet mein dikhani padti hai.</p><p><strong>Report kitne din mein milta hai?</strong> Complete data milne ke baad 5-7 working days. Quarterly 3-5 din.</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

